<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Benefits of Multilingualism – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Multilingual Self</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Multilingualism</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe the process of adapting to a new environment. Interactive: Facilitate peer feedback sessions on presentations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe personal experiences of adapting to a bilingual environment (e.g., "When I started volunteering at the Biomuseo, learning key English phrases helped me communicate better with tourists and coworkers.")
Can facilitate peer feedback on a classmate's presentation about multilingualism by asking questions and providing constructive comments (e.g., "I think your explanation of the benefits of multilingualism was clear. Maybe you could add an example to make it even stronger.")
Can summarize a peer's strengths during a feedback session (e.g., "You did a great job explaining the challenges of learning a second language, and I liked how you used examples from your personal experience.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts and language for the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of people in diverse professional settings (e.g., an international business meeting, a tourism guide, a scientist presenting research). Students are asked to brainstorm how multilingualism might be an asset in these scenarios. Teacher elicits responses like: "It offers more <b>career opportunities</b>," "It enhances global <b>communication</b>," or "You can become more <b>proficient</b> in different fields."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models a sentence to initiate discussion, focusing on reported speech and pronunciation of /s/ and /z/. For example: "A student once <b>said that multilingualism</b> (stress on ‘lin’) opens many doors for <b>success</b> /s/ in <b>business</b> /z/. I <b>believe</b> that <b>fluency</b> in different <b>languages</b> /z/ is a valuable <b>skill</b>." The teacher ensures clear pronunciation of 'multilingualism' and 'communication' with correct word stress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'multilingualism' mean? Is it about speaking one language or more than one?" (Expected answer: "More than one.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When someone says 'it enhances communication,' what does 'enhance' mean? Does it make communication better or worse?" (Expected answer: "Better.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 8-10 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Multilingual Self."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two young adults discussing their experiences adapting to new environments and the role of their language skills. The dialogue includes opinions expressed using reported speech, predictions with future simple, and modals of ability, along with target vocabulary like 'proficiency', 'opportunities', 'bilingual', 'multicultural', 'communicate', and 'excel'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Discussing language diversity: "Multilingualism allows us to connect with people from different cultures and shows respect for their background." Promoting cultural inclusiveness: "If we all learn even a little of each other's languages, we can make our community more inclusive and understanding."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue twice.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea the speakers are conveying about multilingualism and adapting to new places?" (Expected: "They are talking about how knowing multiple languages helps people adapt and creates new opportunities.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher presents 3-4 True/False statements based on specific details from the dialogue (e.g., "One speaker <b>said that companies will prioritize</b> hiring only English speakers." - False). Students justify their answers.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 8-10 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking, using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using reported speech (e.g., "She <b>said that multilingualism</b> offers..."), future simple for predictions (e.g., "Companies <b>will prioritize</b> hiring..."), and modals of ability (e.g., "You <b>can become fluent</b>...") with target vocabulary (multilingualism, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, business, education, fluency, skill, said, argued, believe, prioritize, offer, communicate, become, excel, enhance, learn, bilingual, multicultural, effective, necessary, fluent). Emphasis on /s/ and /z/ pronunciation and word stress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Report the Opinion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides 3-4 direct quotes or opinions about multilingualism from the previous lesson's reading or the current dialogue (e.g., "Learning a new language is hard."). Students take turns rephrasing these using reported speech and various reporting verbs (said, argued, believed). For example: "He <b>argued that learning a new language</b> can be challenging, but the <b>opportunities</b> it creates are immense." Teacher monitors pronunciation of /s/ and /z/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Predicting the Future (Semi-Controlled Substitution Drill):</b> In small groups, students use sentence starters to make predictions about the future of multilingualism. They must use the future simple and at least two target vocabulary words. Example sentence starter: "In 10 years, I believe companies will..." Students complete it: "...<b>will prioritize</b> hiring <b>bilingual</b> employees who <b>can communicate effectively</b> in international <b>business</b>." Teacher guides and corrects word stress, especially for "prioritize," "communication," and "international."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Ask and Share Abilities (Pair Interaction):</b> Students interview their partner about their language learning journey or aspirations, using modals of ability. They then report their partner's responses to another pair. Example questions: "What languages <b>can you speak</b>?" "What <b>skill can you enhance</b> by learning another <b>language</b>?" Partner A asks, Partner B responds. Then A reports B's answers: "She <b>said that she can speak</b> Spanish and a little English, and she <b>believes</b> she <b>can enhance her communication skills</b> by becoming more <b>fluent</b> in English."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 15-18 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Multilingual Self."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "My Adaptation Story" presentations and peer feedback sessions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work individually to prepare a short (2-3 minute) spoken presentation about a personal experience of adapting to a new environment (e.g., a new school, volunteering, a new club) where language played a significant role. They should describe the process and the benefits they gained from any linguistic effort or exposure. Presentations should incorporate reported speech, future simple (for predictions about personal growth or future actions), modals of ability, and at least 5 target vocabulary words.
                <br>After each presentation, the presenting student will facilitate a short (1-2 minute) peer feedback session. They will ask classmates for questions and constructive comments on their presentation, practicing the pragmatic function of promoting cultural inclusiveness and offering culturally sensitive advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher's role:</b> Circulate, listen, encourage participation, provide minimal support where needed, and note examples of effective language use and areas for improvement for later whole-class feedback. Ensure students manage feedback constructively.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 10-12 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking fluency, use of target grammar and vocabulary, and effective facilitation of peer feedback. Students will also use a peer assessment checklist to provide feedback to their classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students deliver their "My Adaptation Story" presentations and actively engage in the peer feedback process, both giving and receiving. During the feedback, they demonstrate the ability to ask questions, provide constructive comments, and summarize strengths, aligning with the learning outcomes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the description of adapting to a new environment.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and accurate use of reported speech, future simple, and modals of ability.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective incorporation of target vocabulary (e.g., multilingualism, proficiency, communication, opportunities).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, including /s/, /z/, and correct word stress in multisyllabic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to facilitate peer feedback by asking questions and providing constructive comments.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstration of cultural sensitivity and inclusiveness during feedback.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief reflection:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new vocabulary word or grammar structure you used confidently today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively do you think you facilitated feedback or participated in giving feedback to a classmate?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 4-5 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflecting on the learning process and preparing for the next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What aspects of describing adaptation or giving feedback did you find most challenging, and how could you improve?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing speaking today help you understand the benefits of multilingualism even better?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students in speaking activities and facilitating feedback? (e.g., specific practice drills, presentation format).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to further improve their fluency and accuracy in using reported speech and modals for discussion and feedback? (e.g., more sentence frames for feedback, explicit modeling of /s/ and /z/ in context).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The speaking practice today, especially describing experiences and giving feedback, will be a strong foundation for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition). Students will transition from orally sharing their experiences and opinions to writing structured paragraphs and short essays on the benefits of multilingualism and personal adaptation, incorporating the same target vocabulary and grammar structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 3-4 min]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>